      "title": "Hypothetical VRAM Breakdown for Local LLMs: Weights, Cache, and Overhead",
      "body": "When planning to run LLMs locally, it's useful to conceptually break down VRAM usage. This is a hypothetical exercise, as no specific hardware or models were tested. For a system with a hypothetical 12 GB GPU, understanding these components can aid in pre-computation planning.\n\nFirst, consider the model weights themselves. Larger models naturally require more VRAM. Second, the KV cache is a significant, dynamic consumer. Its size scales with context window length and batch size. For longer contexts, this can become a substantial portion of your VRAM. Finally, account for other runtime allocations. This includes activations, inference-specific overhead from the framework, and any other temporary memory needs during operation.\n\nSeparating these areas helps in estimating feasibility for different models and configurations. It’s important to remember that this is a conceptual framework, not based on measured results. What are your strategies for estimating these different VRAM components before loading a model?",
