      "title": "Conceptual VRAM Budgeting for Local LLMs on a 12 GB GPU: A Checklist Approach",
      "body": "This post outlines a hypothetical approach to VRAM planning for running language models locally, specifically considering a 12 GB GPU. It is crucial to note that this is a conceptual guide and no actual models or hardware were tested.\n\nThe key is to separate VRAM allocations into distinct categories. The first is the model weights themselves, which are often the largest fixed component. The second is the KV cache, which is dynamic and scales with context length and batch size – essential for longer conversations or processing. The third category encompasses other runtime overhead. This can include intermediate activations, framework-specific memory usage, and any other temporary buffers required during inference.\n\nBy conceptually separating these, users can better estimate whether a given model configuration might fit within their VRAM. This hypothetical framework aims to provide a structured way to think about VRAM limitations without relying on specific benchmarks. How do you typically account for these different VRAM consumers in your local LLM setups?",
