      "title": "Hypothetical VRAM Allocation Strategy for Local LLMs (12 GB GPU)",
      "body": "Planning VRAM for local LLMs can be complex. This guide presents a hypothetical checklist, emphasizing that no specific models or hardware were tested. For a system equipped with a hypothetical 12 GB GPU, a structured approach is beneficial.\n\nWe can divide VRAM requirements into three primary areas: \n1.  **Model Weights:** The static size of the model parameters.\n2.  **KV Cache:** Dynamically allocated memory for key-value pairs, heavily influenced by context length and batch size. This is often a significant factor for longer sequences.\n3.  **Other Runtime Allocations:** This includes activations, framework overhead (e.g., PyTorch, TensorFlow), and any temporary buffers needed during inference.\n\nUnderstanding these distinct components allows for a more informed estimation of VRAM needs. For instance, a model might have smaller weights but require substantial KV cache space for a large context window. This remains a hypothetical framework, not based on measured performance. What factors do you prioritize when estimating VRAM for a new LLM configuration?",
